@node What is Eightbol
@section What is Eightbol

EIGHTBOL is a compiler system  designed to translate programs written in
various high-level  languages into  efficient assembly  code for  a wide
range of 8-bit and 16-bit  microprocessors. Originally developed for the
game @i{Phantasia}, EIGHTBOL,  the EIGHT-Bit-Oriented-Language, provides
a unified  compilation pipeline that supports  multiple source languages
while   maintaining   consistent   code  generation   across   different
target architectures.

@node Front-ends
@section Front-ends

EIGHTBOL supports twenty distinct front-end languages, each with its own
syntax  and semantics,  but  all translating  to  a common  intermediate
representation (the Abstract  Syntax Tree or AST)  before being compiled
to target-specific assembly:

@table @asis
@item AGI —  Adventure Game Interpreter language (Jeff  Stephenson & Ken
Williams)
@item  BASIC   —  Beginners'   All-purpose  Symbolic   Instruction  Code
 (John G. Kemeny and Thomas E. Kurtz, Dartmouth University)
@item BurgerMistress — the Thief of Fate interactive system (Rebecca
Ann ``Burger Becky'' Heinemann)
@item COBOL  — COmmon Business  Oriented Language (Dr RAdm  Grace Hopper)
@item Forth (Chuck Moore)
@item FORTRAN — autmatic FORmula TRANslator (John Backus)
@item  Fountain  —  screenplay  format (as  redesigned  by  Bruce-Robert
Pocock)
@item Garry — Garry Kitchen's GameMaker
@item GOAL — Game-Oriented Assembly Lisp (Andy Gavin)
@item Lingo — MacroMind/Macromedia Director Lingo script (John Henry Thompson)
@item Lua (TecGraf at PUC-Rio)
@item Muddle — MIT Design Language “MDL” (MIT Project MAC)
@item Objective-C — like C, but with objects (Tom Love & Brad Cox)
@item Pascal (Niklaus Wirth)
@item SCI — Sierra Creative Interpreter language (Jeff Stephenson)
@item SCUMM — Script Creation Utility for Maniac Mansion (Ron Gilbert & Aric Wilmunder)
@item SmallTalk (Alan Kay)
@item ZIL — Zork Implementation Language (Marc Blank & Joel Berez)
@end table

@node Back-ends
@section Back-ends

EIGHTBOL targets thirteen different microprocessor architectures, each
with its own code generation backend:

@table @asis
@item 6502 — MOS Technology 6502
@item 65c02 — Western Design Center 65c02
@item 65c816 — Western Design Center 65c816
@item HuC6280 — Hudson Soft HuC6280
@item RP2A03 — Ricoh RP2A03 (used in NES)
@item cp1610 — General Instrument CP1610 (used in Intellivision)
@item Z80 — Zilog Z80
@item SM83 — Sharp SM83 (used in Game Boy)
@item m6800 — Motorola 6800
@item m68k — Motorola 68000 family
@item i286 — Intel 80286
@item ARM7 — ARM Advanced RISC Machines ARM7
@item F8 — Fairchild F8
@item Stack — stack-based back-end
@item JVM — Java Virtual Machine bytecodes
@item wasm — Web Assembly
@item zork — Zork/Infocom Z-Machine
@item p — USCD P-machine
@end table

@node Invoking EIGHTBOL
@section Invoking EIGHTBOL

The EIGHTBOL compiler is invoked from the command line as follows:

@example
bin/eightbol @var{options} @var{source-file}
@end example

Where @var{options} can include:

@table @asis
@item -l @var{language}
Specify the  source language (agi, basic,  burgermistress, cobol, forth,
fortran, fountain, garry, goal, lingo, lua, muddle, objective, pascal, sci,
scumm, smalltalk, zil)
@item -c @var{cpu}
Specify the target CPU (6502, 65c02, 65c816, huc6280, rp2a03, cp1610,
z80, sm83, m6800, m68k, i286, arm7, f8, stack, zork, wasm, jvm, p)
@item -o @var{output-file}
Specify the output assembly file
@item -I @var{path}
Add @var{path} to the copybook search path
@item -v
Enable verbose output
@item --help
Display help information
@end table

@node Using in a Makefile
@section Using in a Makefile

EIGHTBOL integrates seamlessly with Make-based build systems. A typical
Makefile rule for compiling a EIGHTBOL source file looks like this:

@example
%.asm: %.agi
	bin/eightbol -l agi -c 6502 -o $@ $<
@end example

For projects with multiple source files targeting different CPUs, you can
use pattern rules with automatic variables:

@example
OBJECTS = main.asm loader.asm sprite.asm

all: $(OBJECTS)

%.asm: %.cob
	bin/eightbol -l cobol -c 6502 -o $@ $<

%.asm: %.lua
	bin/eightbol -l lua -c arm7 -o $@ $<
@end example

Additional Makefile variables can be defined for common options:

@example
EIGHTBOL = bin/eightbol
CPU = 6502
LANG = cobol
COPYPATH = copysrc

%.asm: %.cob
	$(EIGHTBOL) -l $(LANG) -c $(CPU) -I $(COPYPATH) -o $@ $<
@end example

